Id: sum002f
Severity: FAIL
Category: boot
Check: check_single
Fix: Remove the unit or drop-in in /etc that replaces ExecStart=, so the service runs sulogin again (systemctl cat UNIT shows what it is made of).
References: sulogin(8); systemd.special(7); systemd.unit(5); systemd.service(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-3, IA-2; ISO 8.5, 8.9; CE Secure configuration

systemd's rescue.service or emergency.service starts a shell without
sulogin. These are what rescue mode (rescue.target, "single" or
systemd.unit=rescue.target on the kernel command line) and emergency
mode (emergency.target, also entered on its own when a file system in
fstab cannot be mounted at boot) run, as root, on the console. As
systemd ships them they run systemd-sulogin-shell, which runs sulogin
and asks for root's password; older releases ran sulogin through
sh -c. Without it, anyone at the console, or with a virtual machine's
console, who can choose the kernel command line or make the boot fail
gets a root shell without a password.

The unit file and its drop-ins are read as systemd reads them: the
first unit of the name in /etc/systemd/system, /run/systemd/system or
the vendor directories, then the .conf drop-ins of its .d directories.
The usual cause is a drop-in that empties ExecStart= and sets a shell
(ExecStart=-/bin/sh), added to get into a machine whose root password
was lost. Remove it, or mask the unit if rescue mode is not wanted.
